## What happened (in order)

### 1. embodied-schemas#81 -- EPYC 9754 (Bergamo) multi-die rewrite

Sprint #245 PR 4. Re-authored Bergamo from a single virtual die to a
proper 2-die layout. **First exercise of the shared-IOD reuse pattern**:
Bergamo's `genoa_iod` die is byte-identical to EPYC 9654's
(same `die_id`, area, transistor count, silicon_bin decomposition,
IOBlock field values). The only delta from 9654 at the silicon level
is the compute die: 8 Zen 4c CCDs (vs 9654's 12 Zen 4 CCDs).

- Compute die: `epyc_9754_compute_aggregate`, TSMC N5, 581.6 mm² / 77.6 B tx
- IO die: reused `genoa_iod`, TSMC N6, 397 mm² / 12 B tx
- 8 IFOP point-to-point coherent links on the compute die

Two new cross-SKU shared-IOD invariant tests pin that the IOD silicon
is byte-identical between 9654 and 9754.

Headline PhysicalSpec sums preserved: 978.6 mm² / 89.6 B tx.

29 new tests; 947/947 full repo pass (+15 net).

### 2. embodied-schemas#82 -- EPYC 9965 (Turin Dense) multi-die rewrite

Sprint #245 PR 5. **First NEW IOD silicon under the v13 schema** --
distinct from the Genoa IOD shared by 9654/9754. Turin IOD introduces:

- DDR5-6000 IMC (vs Genoa's DDR5-4800) -- 576 GB/s peak
- CXL 2.0 (vs CXL 1.1) -- adds memory pooling
- ~13 B transistors (vs ~12 B) -- larger IMC PHY + IF routers

Compute die: `epyc_9965_compute_aggregate`, TSMC N4P placeholder
(N3E pending), 876 mm² / 132 B tx; 12 Zen 5c CCDs.

Two new cross-SKU distinct-IOD invariant tests pin that Turin IOD is
silicon-level distinct from Genoa IOD (different die_id, different
memory BW, different CXL version, different transistor count) while
sharing the family (Infinity Fabric, ARM PSP, 12-channel DDR5, 128
PCIe Gen5, 4x G-link, TSMC N6).

The v13 IOBlock schema now has coverage against two distinct IOD
silicon families. Schema generalized cleanly: no field-level changes
needed for Turin; only data deltas.

30 new tests; 962/962 full repo pass (+15 net). IOBlock catalog pin
bumped from 2 SKUs to 3 -- closes the IOBlock data-side sprint.

